    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='Security Misconfiguration with Spring Boot Actuator',
         body=None,
         narration='Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains misconfiguration with Actuator, in Java. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] Spring Boot Actuator is the part of a store that was built to be looked into, so a stranger who reaches it can read the payment key, list the unlisted routes, download the heap and stop the store; Spring Boot hides values and keeps the dangerous endpoints off by default, and the rest is the store\'s job: another port, a short list, login rules, read-only access and a checker that runs on every change. [[slnc 600]] Think of it this way. [[slnc 300]] A shop where the staff-only door has a sign that says "S T A F F" and no lock, and behind it hang the safe\'s combination, the plan of every back room and a camera feed of the stockroom. The sign is a setting. The lock is another one. [[slnc 700]] In this video, the domain is an online store. [[slnc 500]] By the end, you will be able to: [[slnc 300]] Name what each Actuator endpoint gives a stranger. [[slnc 300]] Explain what Spring Boot hides by default and what it cannot hide. [[slnc 300]] Separate operations from customers by port, list, login rules and access level.'),
